# {{WidgetName}}

A pinnable webapp using the public `UI.WebBrowser` element. Requires a host supporting protocol 81.

The host owns rendering, controller input, cookies, website permissions, and browser lifetime. This widget does not receive page text, tokens, or browser objects. No website-specific scripts are generated.

## Configuration

Edit the `WebBrowserDocument` in `src/{{WidgetName}}.cs`:

- `Url`: starting HTTPS page. Keep `Id` stable and advance `NavigationId` only for an intentional new navigation, never on render.
- `Options.Chrome`: `OnDemand` gives the page full space; LS opens browser controls. `Toolbar` keeps controls visible.
- `Options.Storage`: `Persistent` retains website login between app runs; `Private` does not. Login is isolated from other widgets and desktop browsers.
- `Options.ProfileKey`: bounded lowercase account key, for example `personal` or `work`. Different elements with the same widget/account/storage identity share website state. This is not a filesystem path.
- `Options.ExternalLinks`: `OtherOriginsInWindows` opens ordinary user-selected offsite links in Windows. This is not an origin allowlist: authentication redirects can cross origins. Use `InWidget` for ordinary navigation within the element.
- `Options.Popups`: the preset uses `Hosted` to preserve authentication popups' opener and shared profile. `ReplacePage` navigates the current page; `SystemBrowser` opens HTTP(S) popup URLs in Windows, without shared cookies or opener. Popup routing is independent of ordinary links.
- `Options.Background`: the webapp preset uses `KeepAlive` for bounded background residency. The host holds the owning worker's residency automatically; no matching manifest residency setting is needed. Use `Automatic` for pages that can be unloaded while hidden. Users can stop a session from website controls or the tray menu. This is not an authorization to use the microphone or camera.
- `BrowserInteractionMode.InteractOnFocus`: controller interaction starts on focus; B follows framework navigation. Use `ActivateToInteract` when the page sits among other controls.

The manifest enables ordinary full-widget pinning. Website features and authentication depend on WebView2 compatibility and should be tested against the real site. Do not assume a successful page load proves voice, screen sharing, or account recovery.

Keep a stable browser declaration published while its background session is needed. Removing that document, disabling the widget, or stopping its session closes the page and ends its connections. Multiple documents with the same widget/account/storage share website data; changing account or storage recreates the browser controller. Neither author code nor widget IPC receives that data.

LS reveals the native controls. Menu opens page actions; B closes a transient toolbar or hosted popup before following normal framework navigation. Website permissions, clear-data, text prompts, file uploads/downloads and session controls are host UI. Windows file pickers currently require mouse/keyboard. Notifications require a running page; push while WidgetRail is closed is unsupported. Never generate site-specific DOM scripts or implement cookie handling in the widget.

## Build and test

The generated project includes the local SDK feed. Build `{{WidgetName}}.csproj`, then use `wrail preview`, `wrail validate`, and `wrail pack` as with other widgets. `widgetrail.scenarios.json` supplies a deterministic declarative preview; it does not log into the website.

## Notifications and activity

Ordinary widgets can use `HostServices.Notifications` with bounded manifest
`notificationCategories`. Introduce optional categories during setup and call
`ConfigureAsync(categoryId)` from an action to open shared host preferences. Do
not store a duplicate enabled/opacity choice. Use `UpdateAsync` for metadata or
progress without replaying a banner, and a declared `OpenIntent` for a pending
result destination. `SourceHidden` lets the host suppress redundant results when
the owner is already visible. Notifications do not extend worker lifetime.

Report independent `HostServices.Activity.SetAsync(sourceId, activity)` sources
only for activity the widget owns; clearing one source leaves the others intact.
WebView audio and website notifications are detected by the host automatically;
webapp wrappers need no DOM polling or mirrored status reporting. Website notices
use the General category and their original website activation callback. Their
banners are eligible with the overlay open or hidden (Always); no widget option
or custom notification forwarding is needed. Website permission, saved host
preferences, quiet intervals and available screen space still apply.

Keep stable focus IDs and declare navigation/Back destinations. The host preserves
widget focus through snapshot replacement; notification publication must not
change the widget's route or force focus.

Declare a notification category's `retention` as `bannerOnly` for transient notices
or `pending` for notices kept in the list. The host enforces this declaration and
shows useful controls: On/Off for banner-only categories, or Banner + list / List
only / Off for pending categories. Omitting `retention` allows both kinds for
existing categories. Do not advertise pending-list choices for song banners.
